# 0. Instructions for the developer / Claude

Build a real, working, full-stack web application based on this specification. Do not create only a landing page, static mockup, or frontend demo. The result must include a functioning backend, database, authentication, email verification, user profiles, requests, matching, notifications, offers, messaging, job lifecycle, reviews, admin controls, and responsive user interface.

The app name, logo, colours, typography, and theme are intentionally undecided. Use neutral, clean placeholder branding and centralize all brand settings so they can be changed later without rewriting the app.

**Important implementation rules:**

1. Do not invent missing product rules. Follow this document. Where a decision is marked **RECOMMENDED DEFAULT**, implement that default and document it.
2. Do not show fake user counts, fake jobs, fake reviews, fake online users, or fake testimonials in production. Any demo records must be clearly labelled as demo/sample data and must be removable.
3. Do not leave buttons or screens non-functional. If a feature is not included in the first release, hide it or label it clearly as “Coming later”; do not make it appear functional.
4. Never put secret keys, database passwords, OAuth secrets, email credentials, or service-role keys in frontend code or the ZIP. Use environment variables and provide `.env.example` with placeholder values only.
5. Enforce critical rules on the server/database, not only in the browser. For example, an email-domain check, one-account-per-email rule, one-review-per-job rule, request expiry, and permissions must not depend only on frontend validation.
6. Use database constraints, server-side authorization, and row-level security or equivalent protections wherever supported.
7. The final ZIP must contain the complete source code, database schema/migrations, seed script, tests, `.env.example`, setup instructions, deployment instructions, and a clear README.
8. Before delivery, run the application, test the main flows, fix errors, and provide a list of what works and what still needs external credentials or production setup.

