# 4. Profile system

Each student has a profile that acts as a student skill profile and mini professional portfolio.

## 4.1 Required profile details

- Display name.
- Verified university email (private; do not display the full email publicly by default).
- University/institution.
- Campus.
- Department.
- Degree/programme (where relevant).
- Current semester / year.
- Profile image or avatar (optional).
- Short bio / introduction.
- Skill categories and skill tags.
- Work preferences (online, on-campus, in-person, or either).
- Preferred payment methods (informational only).
- Availability schedule.
- Optional preferred working arrangement/gender preference settings.

The user should be able to edit profile details when their semester or department information changes, while retaining historical job records.

## 4.2 Skills and tags

Skills must use a searchable, standardized tag system. Do not rely only on free-text bios.

Example categories and tags:

**Programming & Technology**
- Java, C++, Python, JavaScript, HTML, CSS, React, Web Development, Databases, Debugging, PC Troubleshooting, Software Setup.

**Graphic Design & UI**
- Photoshop, Illustrator, Canva, Logo Design, Poster Design, Presentation Design, UI/UX, Figma, Branding.

**Video & Media**
- Video Editing, Reels/Shorts, Premiere Pro, After Effects, CapCut, Motion Graphics, Photography, Event Photography, Videography.

**Academic Help**
- Mathematics, Programming Tutoring, OOP, Discrete Structures, Notes, Study Guidance, Exam Preparation, Concept Explanation.

**Writing & Communication**
- Content Writing, Proofreading, Translation, Copywriting, CV Writing, Report Formatting, Presentation Writing.

**Business & Other**
- Accounting, Excel, Marketing, Social Media Management, Data Entry, Research Assistance, Event Support, Other.

Administrators can add, merge, rename, or deactivate tags. Users can suggest a new tag if it does not exist.

## 4.3 Portfolio

Users can add examples of work they have done.

Each portfolio item may include:
- Title.
- Description.
- Skill tags.
- Image/video/document preview or external link, subject to file rules.
- Completion date or approximate date.
- Optional client permission/credit statement.
- Whether the work was done through this platform or independently.

Clearly distinguish:
- **Verified platform work:** linked to a completed job record.
- **Personal portfolio work:** uploaded or linked by the user and not independently verified by the platform.

Do not display private client information or private course material without permission.

## 4.4 Availability and online status

Profiles should show, where the user permits:
- Online now / offline.
- Last active time (e.g. “Active 12 minutes ago”).
- Preferred available days and time ranges.
- Work mode: online, in-person/on campus, or either.

Users must be able to hide online status and last-seen information. Do not expose exact activity details to people the user has blocked.

Availability must be separated into two data types:
1. **User-set schedule:** the user manually chooses when they are usually available.
2. **Observed activity/work pattern:** statistics derived from actual platform activity, such as usual response periods. Label these as estimates, not guaranteed availability.

## 4.5 Profile work graph and statistics

Show an attractive, understandable work summary based only on real platform records.

Example profile summary:

- **4.86 / 5 average rating**
- **37 verified reviews**
- **37 completed jobs**
- **31 unique clients**
- **6 departments served**
- **12 repeat clients**
- **94% on-time completion** (only when enough reliable records exist)

The statistics must be calculated from the database, not typed in by users.

Suggested graphs:
- Completed jobs by month.
- Average rating trend by month (show the number of reviews behind each period).
- Most requested skills.
- User-set weekly availability.
- Typical response-time ranges, if enough data exists.

Do not infer availability from a single login. Do not show a percentage if there is insufficient data. Define every statistic clearly in a tooltip or help text.

## 4.6 Preferred payment methods

Users may list the ways they prefer to receive payment:
- Easypaisa.
- JazzCash.
- NayaPay.
- Bank transfer.
- Cash / in person.
- Free / no payment required.
- Other (optional).

The platform does not process, hold, guarantee, or escrow money in the first release. Payment is arranged directly between students outside the app. The profile may list a preferred method, but private account numbers, wallet IDs, or phone numbers must not be exposed publicly. If users choose to share payment details, they should do so privately and at their own risk.

