# 6. Request system — the core feature

A request is a temporary public post explaining what help a student needs.

Examples:
- “I need someone to edit a 2-minute event video.”
- “Can someone explain polymorphism in Java?”
- “I need a photographer for our department event.”
- “Looking for notes for Discrete Structures.”
- “Need help troubleshooting my laptop.”
- “Looking for a teammate for a permitted semester project.”

## 6.1 Create-request form

The form should be clear and easy to complete on mobile.

Required or optional fields:
- Short title (required).
- Description (required).
- Main category (required).
- Skill tags (at least one recommended; required where practical).
- Who can see/respond (required; default can be “eligible members at my campus”).
- Preferred department(s): Any or selected department(s).
- Preferred semester range: Any or a minimum/maximum semester.
- Preferred campus: Any or selected campus.
- Preferred helper gender: Any / Male / Female (optional preference; see Section 12).
- Work mode: Online / In person on campus / Either.
- Preferred availability or deadline (optional).
- Budget: Free / Paid / Negotiable.
- Budget range or amount (optional for paid jobs).
- Preferred payment method (optional; informational only).
- Additional requirements (optional).

The form must explain that payment is arranged directly between users and is not processed by the platform.

## 6.2 Department and semester targeting

The requester can choose who they are looking for:
- Any department.
- One or more specific departments.
- Any semester.
- A semester range (for example, semester 3 to semester 6).
- Any campus or a selected campus.

Example: “Need someone from Software Engineering or Computer Science, semester 4 or above, who knows Java.”

A student should receive a match alert only if their profile satisfies the request's selected filters and has at least one matching skill tag (unless the request is explicitly open to any skill/anyone).

## 6.3 Request feed cards

Each request card should show:
- Request title.
- Short description preview.
- Category and skill tags.
- Requester's display name and profile preview.
- Department, campus, and semester of requester (based on their profile visibility settings).
- Budget/payment status if supplied.
- Preferred helper criteria in a concise form.
- Posted time (e.g. “Posted 12 minutes ago”).
- Time remaining before expiry.
- Request status.
- Number of offers/people interested.
- Buttons to view details, offer help, and report.

Example:

**Need a Video Editor**  
BS Software Engineering · Semester 2 · NUML Rawalpindi  
“Need someone to edit a 2-minute society event video.”  
Tags: Video Editing · Premiere Pro · Reels  
Budget: Rs. 2,000 (example only)  
Posted 12 minutes ago · Expires in 23h 48m  
3 people interested

## 6.4 Request lifetime and expiry

- A request can remain in the public active feed for a maximum of 24 hours from its original posting time.
- Show the age of the request and a visible countdown/time-remaining label.
- When the 24-hour period ends, automatically change its state to **Expired** and remove it from the active feed.
- Do not silently refresh or extend a request's lifetime.
- A user can create a new request after an earlier one expires.
- The owner may manually close/cancel a request sooner.

The expiry job must run server-side (scheduled worker/cron or equivalent). Do not rely on the user's browser being open.

## 6.5 Request alerts and 12-hour inbox rule

When a request is posted, the matching system identifies eligible students and sends an in-app request alert to them.

Each matching alert:
- Appears in the user's **Request Alerts** inbox.
- Shows the request title, skill tags, requester details allowed by privacy settings, time posted, and a link to open the request.
- Remains in that request-alert inbox for 12 hours from delivery.
- Is then automatically removed from the Request Alerts inbox.
- Disappearing from the alert inbox does not delete or expire the actual request.
- The actual request may continue to appear in the public feed until its 24-hour deadline or until its status changes.

Deduplicate alerts so the same user does not receive repeated alerts for the same request because of duplicate matching tags. Do not send an alert to the request creator. Users should be able to mute request alerts or adjust categories/skills they want alerts for.

Separate request-match alerts from important system messages such as private messages, job status changes, security alerts, and account notices. The 12-hour expiry applies specifically to temporary request-match alerts, not necessarily to all messages or security notices.

## 6.6 Matching algorithm

Match request to profiles using the selected criteria:
1. Skill tag match.
2. Department match (if restricted).
3. Semester range match (if restricted).
4. Campus match (if restricted).
5. Preferred helper gender (if selected and voluntarily provided by the member).
6. Work mode and availability (if selected).
7. Account active and email verified.
8. User has not blocked the requester and is not blocked by them.

The system should allow “Any” for filters. A user should not receive a notification for a request that excludes their department, semester, campus, or selected preference.

Do not rank people only by star rating. Search/matching can prioritize relevance and availability, while showing reputation transparently.

## 6.7 Offers and visible interest

Several people can respond to the same request before the requester selects one.

An offer should display:
- Person's profile name and image/avatar.
- Department, semester, and campus.
- Matching skill tags.
- Average rating and completed jobs.
- Short offer message (e.g. “I can edit this; I have done similar event videos”).
- Availability/estimated delivery time if supplied.
- View Profile button.
- Message button.
- Accept Offer button (visible to request owner only).

The requester and other eligible members who can view the request should be able to see the public offer/comment thread, including which students are willing to help. This matches the intended community feel: if five students offer help, viewers can see those five responses and compare their visible profile summaries. Each public offer should show the student's name/avatar, department, semester, relevant tags, rating/review count, completed-job count, and a short offer message.

Use a structured “Offer help” action that creates a visible offer/comment card rather than an uncontrolled free-form chat thread. Keep the public response focused on availability and relevant experience. Private details, phone numbers, wallet IDs, personal email addresses, and longer discussions must stay in private in-app messages. The requester alone can accept one offer. Add reporting and moderation controls to the public thread, plus rate limits to prevent spam.

## 6.8 Selecting a helper and processing state

When the requester accepts one offer:
- Create a unique job record.
- Change the request status from **Active** to **Processing**.
- Temporarily remove the request from the public active-request feed so new people do not keep applying.
- Keep the request visible to its owner and selected helper in their job/request details.
- Notify the selected helper and requester.
- Close or mark other pending offers as not selected.
- Start the in-app job conversation if one does not already exist.

For the first version, one request should have one selected helper. Multi-helper jobs can be considered later.

## 6.9 If a job is declined or cancelled during processing

If the selected helper declines before work begins or cannot continue:
- The requester can cancel the selected job.
- The request returns to **Active** if time remains before its original 24-hour expiry.
- The request returns to the feed with its original remaining time; do not restart the 24-hour clock automatically.
- Previously interested people may be notified that the request is active again, subject to notification settings.
- If the original expiry time has already passed, the request becomes **Expired** instead.

If the requester cancels the request, mark it **Cancelled** and remove it from the active feed.

If a job is already in progress and there is a disagreement, use the dispute/report flow instead of allowing either side to rewrite the history.

## 6.10 Job completion

Suggested lifecycle:

`Active → Offer(s) Received → Processing → Work Delivered → Completed`

Alternative outcomes:

`Processing → Cancelled / Declined → Active again (only if time remains) or Expired`

Detailed completion flow:
1. Helper marks work as **Delivered / Ready for Review**.
2. Requester receives a notification and reviews the work.
3. Requester can accept completion or raise a problem/dispute.
4. To close a normal successful job as **Completed**, requester must submit a star rating for the helper.
5. Written comment is optional.
6. After rating submission, mark the job Completed, remove the request from the public feed permanently, and update statistics.
7. Each completed job creates exactly one verified review from the requester to the helper.

**RECOMMENDED SAFEGUARD:** If the requester does not respond after reminders, provide an escalation path for support/admin review. Do not automatically force a positive review. Allow admin to resolve the record with an explicit status such as “Closed after review” or “Cancelled/disputed” so statistics remain honest.

